## What is Ruby Raider?

Ruby Raider is a generator and scaffolding gem to make UI test automation easier.

### Supported Frameworks

| Web Testing Framework | Mobile Testing Framework           |
|-----------------------|------------------------------------|
| RSpec and Selenium    | RSpec and Appium for iOS           |
|                       | RSpec and Appium for Android       |
|                       | RSpec and Appium Cross-platform    |

**Optional add-on for web projects:**
- **Accessibility testing (axe)**: `--accessibility` flag

***The web tests run against the [Raider Test Store](https://raider-test-site.onrender.com/).***

***To run Appium tests, download the example [app](https://github.com/RaiderHQ/raider_test_app) and start the server:***

```bash
raider u start_appium
```

This works on all platforms (Mac OS, Linux and Windows).

## Getting Started

```bash
gem install ruby_raider
raider new [name_of_project]
```

Or with parameters:

```bash
raider new [name_of_project] -p framework:[framework] automation:[automation_type]
```

### Commands

```
raider generate         # Scaffolding commands
raider utility          # Utility commands
raider new [NAME]       # Create new project
raider version          # Show version
raider help [COMMAND]   # Describe commands
```

Shortcuts: `g` (generate), `n` (new), `u` (utility), `v` (version)

### Scaffolding

```
raider g page [NAME]      # Create page object
raider g spec [NAME]      # Create RSpec test
raider g scaffold [NAME]  # Create page + test
```

<%- test_cmd = 'rspec' -%>
## How the framework works

This section walks through the project: where things live, how a test finds an element and acts on it, and how to
<%- if examples? -%>
run the tests. Most examples come from the files in this project.
<%- else -%>
run the tests.

This project was generated without example tests, so its <%= mobile? ? 'pages' : 'pages, components' %> and tests folders are empty.
Grow it with the scaffolding commands above: `raider g scaffold checkout` writes a `Checkout` page and its
spec, and `raider g page` and `raider g spec` write one file at a time. The code examples
below show what goes in them.
<%- end -%>

Two Ruby words you will see below: a **class** is a named group of methods, and a **method** is what other
languages call a function (it starts with `def` and ends with `end`).

### Folder layout

- `config/`: the settings the tests read.
<%- if mobile? -%>
  `capabilities.yml` describes the device and the app<%= cross_platform? ? ', and `config.yml` holds the other settings' : '' %>.
  See "Settings" below.
<%- if examples? -%>
- `page_objects/pages/`: one class per screen of the app. `home.rb` is the product list and `pdp.rb` the product
  details page. A page holds the locators of its elements and the actions a test can do on that screen.
<%- else -%>
- `page_objects/pages/`: one class per screen of the app, empty for now. A page holds the locators of its elements
  and the actions a test can do on that screen.
<%- end -%>
- `page_objects/abstract/`: `page.rb`, the base class every page builds on. It requires the Raider runtime and includes
  `gesture_helper.rb`.
- `helpers/`: `driver_helper.rb` starts the Appium session, `gesture_helper.rb` has `swipe`, `scroll` and `scroll_to`,
  and `video_helper.rb` records the screen<%= cross_platform? ? '. `appium_helper.rb` has `element(ios: …, android: …)`, a lookup for both platforms' : '' %>.
  `spec_helper.rb` sets up RSpec: it starts the app before each test and closes it after.
<%- if examples? -%>
- `spec/`: the tests. `pdp_page_spec.rb` opens a product and checks its "Add to Cart" button.
<%- else -%>
- `spec/`: the tests, empty for now.
<%- end -%>
<%- else -%>
  `config.yml` is described under "Settings" below.
<%- if examples? -%>
- `page_objects/pages/`: one class per page of the site. `login.rb` is the login page and `account.rb` the account
  page. A page holds the locators of its elements and the actions a test can do on that page.
- `page_objects/components/`: parts that appear on many pages, such as a header. Add one with `raider g component`.
<%- else -%>
- `page_objects/pages/`: one class per page of the site, empty for now. A page holds the locators of its elements
  and the actions a test can do on that page.
- `page_objects/components/`: parts that appear on many pages, such as a header. Add one with `raider g component`.
<%- end -%>
- `page_objects/abstract/`: `page.rb` and `component.rb`, the base classes every page and component builds on.
  `page.rb` requires the Raider runtime and gives each page `by_role`.
- `helpers/`: `driver_helper.rb` starts the browser, `video_helper.rb` records failure videos and `session_state.rb`
  reuses the login. `spec_helper.rb` sets up RSpec: it opens the browser before each test and closes it after.
<%- if examples? -%>
- `models/`: test data. `data/users.yml` holds the users, and `model_factory.rb` loads them.
<%- end -%>
<%- if examples? -%>
- `spec/`: the tests. `login_page_spec.rb` logs in with the right password and with a wrong one.
<%- else -%>
- `spec/`: the tests, empty for now.
<%- end -%>
<%- end -%>
<%- if azure? && !mobile? -%>
- `azure-pipelines.yml`: the CI pipeline.
<%- elsif !azure? -%>
- `.github/workflows/`: the CI pipeline.
<%- end -%>

### Settings

<%- if mobile? -%>
<%- if cross_platform? -%>
`config/capabilities.yml` tells Appium which device to use and which app to install. The capabilities for each
platform sit under the `ios:` and `android:` parts. In each part, `platformName` sits at the top, and the rest sits
under `appium:options`: `platformVersion`, `deviceName`, `automationName` (`XCUITest` on iOS, `UiAutomator2` on
Android) and `app`, the path to the app file.
<%- else -%>
`config/capabilities.yml` tells Appium which device to use and which app to install. `platformName` sits at the
top, and the rest sits under `appium:options`: `platformVersion`, `deviceName`, `automationName` (`XCUITest` on
iOS, `UiAutomator2` on Android) and `app`, the path to the app file.
<%- end -%>
The `browserstack` part is used instead when you run with `TEST_ENV=browserstack`.

<%- if cross_platform? -%>
`config/config.yml`:

- `platform`: which part of `capabilities.yml` to use, `ios` or `android`.
- `timeout`: how many seconds finds, actions and assertions wait before the test fails (10). To wait longer,
  change it, for example to `timeout: 15`.
- `strict` (`true`) and `actionability` (`true`): see [Waiting](#waiting). `raider u strict off` and
  `raider u actionability off` set them. `RAIDER_TIMEOUT`, `RAIDER_STRICT` and `RAIDER_ACTIONABILITY` override the file for one run.
- `video`: failure videos, see [Video replay](#video-replay).
<%- else -%>
This project has no `config/config.yml`. The runtime waits up to 10 seconds, with strict and actionability on. To change
that for one run, set `RAIDER_TIMEOUT`, `RAIDER_STRICT` or `RAIDER_ACTIONABILITY`, for example `RAIDER_TIMEOUT=15`. To
change it for the project, create `config/config.yml` with the keys you want:

```yaml
timeout: 15
strict: true
actionability: true
```
<%- end -%>
<%- else -%>
`config/config.yml`:

- `browser`: the browser the tests run in, `chrome` or `firefox`.
<%- if examples? -%>
- `url`: the address of the site under test. Each page adds its own path to it (`Login` adds
  `index.php?rt=account/login`).
<%- else -%>
- `url`: the address of the site under test. Each page adds its own path to it. Change it to your site.
<%- end -%>
- `timeout`: how many seconds finds, actions and assertions wait before the test fails (10). `RAIDER_TIMEOUT`, `RAIDER_STRICT` and
  `RAIDER_ACTIONABILITY` override `timeout`, `strict` and `actionability` for one run.
- `strict`: `true` makes `find_element` fail when a locator matches more than one element. `false` returns the first.
  `raider u strict off` sets it.
- `actionability`: `true` makes actions wait until the element is ready, see
  [Waiting and actionability](#waiting-and-actionability). `raider u actionability off` sets it.
  `RAIDER_TIMEOUT`, `RAIDER_STRICT` and `RAIDER_ACTIONABILITY` override the file for one run.
- `viewport`: the page viewport's width and height in CSS pixels.
- `browser_arguments`: extra options passed to each browser when it starts.
- `headless`: not in the file by default. Add `headless: true`, or set `HEADLESS=true` for one run, to run the
  browser without a window.
- `video`: failure videos, see [Video replay](#video-replay).
<%- if trace_helper? -%>
- `trace`: failing-test traces, see [Trace replay](#trace-replay).
<%- end -%>
- `login_state`: how long a saved login is reused, see [Reusing the login](#reusing-the-login).
<%- end -%>

### Pages and components

A page keeps two kinds of methods: **elements**, which say where something is, and **actions**, which do something
<%- if examples? -%>
with it. From `page_objects/pages/<%= mobile? ? 'home.rb' : 'login.rb' %>`:
<%- else -%>
with it. For example, on a <%= mobile? ? 'product list screen' : 'login page' %>:
<%- end -%>

```ruby
<%- if mobile? -%>
def go_to_product_detail
  first_product.click
end

def first_product
<%- if cross_platform? -%>
  element({ ios: { accessibility_id: 'product-item-1' }, android: { accessibility_id: 'product-item-1' } })
<%- else -%>
  driver.find_element(accessibility_id: 'product-item-1')
<%- end -%>
end
<%- else -%>
def login(username, password)
  username_field.send_keys(username)
  password_field.send_keys(password)
  login_button.click
end

def username_field
  driver.find_element(id: 'loginFrm_loginname')
end
<%- end -%>
```

Pages call <%= mobile? ? 'Appium' : 'Selenium' %> directly: `driver.find_element` finds an element and `click` and `send_keys` act on it.
The Raider runtime (`ruby_raider/runtime`) makes them wait. `helpers/driver_helper.rb` and `page_objects/abstract/page.rb`
require it, so it is loaded before the driver exists.
<%- if mobile? -%>
<%- if cross_platform? -%>

Because this project runs on both iOS and Android, a locator can name one for each: `element(ios: { … }, android: { … })`
from `helpers/appium_helper.rb` picks the one for the device the test runs on. It calls `driver.find_element`, so it waits
like any other find.
<%- end -%>
<%- else -%>

<%- if examples? -%>
A **component** is a part of the page that has its own methods. It builds on `page_objects/abstract/component.rb`. This
project has none yet; add one with `raider g component`.
<%- else -%>
A **component** is a part of the page that has its own methods. It builds on `page_objects/abstract/component.rb`, which
keeps the element it wraps as `component`, so `component.find_element(...)` searches inside it.
<%- end -%>
<%- end -%>

### Locators

<%- if mobile? -%>
An element method returns a **locator**: a Ruby hash (a key and a value in curly braces) that tells Appium how to find
the element. `{ accessibility_id: 'product-item-1' }` means "the element whose accessibility id is `product-item-1`".
These keys work on an app: `accessibility_id:`, `id:`, `xpath:` and `class_name:`.

```ruby
driver.find_element(accessibility_id: 'product-item-1')
driver.find_elements(accessibility_id: 'product-item-1')
```

`find_element` returns the first match. `find_elements` returns every match.
<%- else -%>
An element method returns a **locator**: a Ruby hash (a key and a value in curly braces) that tells Selenium how to find
the element. `{ id: 'loginFrm_loginname' }` means "the element whose id is `loginFrm_loginname`". Selenium's own keys
work: `id:`, `css:`, `xpath:`, `name:`, `class:` and `link_text:`. Raider adds the ones people see on the page:

| Key | Finds | Example |
|-----|-------|---------|
| `label:` | the field with that label text, `aria-label` or `aria-labelledby` | `driver.find_element(label: 'Email')` |
| `placeholder:` | the field with that placeholder | `driver.find_element(placeholder: 'Search')` |
| `alt:` | the element with that `alt` text, such as an image | `driver.find_element(alt: 'Logo')` |
| `title:` | the element with that `title` | `driver.find_element(title: 'Close')` |
| `text:` | the innermost element whose text contains it | `driver.find_element(text: 'Sign in')` |
| `test_id:` | the element whose `data-testid` equals it | `driver.find_element(test_id: 'submit')` |

`label:`, `placeholder:`, `alt:`, `title:` and `text:` match a part of the text, ignoring extra spaces. For a match of the
whole text, pass the value and `{ exact: true }` in an array:

```ruby
driver.find_element(label: ['Email', { exact: true }])
```

`test_id:` reads `data-testid`. If your app uses another attribute, set `Raider.test_id_attribute = 'data-qa'` or
`RAIDER_TEST_ID_ATTRIBUTE=data-qa`.

`by_role` finds an element the way a person or a screen reader does, by its ARIA role and its name.
<%- if examples? -%>
`login.rb` uses it for the button:
<%- else -%>
For example, a button:
<%- end -%>

```ruby
driver.find_element(by_role('button', name: 'Login'))
```

`by_role(role, name:, exact:, include_hidden:, level:)` takes these options:

- `name:` is the accessible name, matched as a part of the text. `exact: true` matches all of it.
- `include_hidden: true` also finds elements that are not visible (by default they are skipped).
- `level:` picks a heading by its level, 1 to 6.

```ruby
driver.find_element(by_role('button', name: 'Save', exact: true))
driver.find_element(by_role('heading', level: 1))
driver.find_element(by_role('dialog', include_hidden: true))
```

An unknown role raises an `ArgumentError`. The roles are the ARIA role names, such as `button`, `link` and `textbox`.
<%- end -%>

**Strict.** `find_element` fails with `Raider::AmbiguousMatch` when the locator matches more than one element. The message
lists the first three, so you can narrow the locator. When you want every match, use `driver.find_elements`: it never fails
on many matches. <%= mobile? ? 'Strict is off on native app screens, where `find_element` returns the first match.' : '`raider u strict off` turns strict off for the project.' %>

<%- if mobile? -%>
### Waiting

Apps load at their own speed, so the runtime waits for you. It checks every 0.1 seconds, for up to `timeout` seconds (10).

- `find_element` and `find_elements` wait for a match. `find_element` fails with a message such as
  `no such element: { id: "x" } (waited 10s)`. `find_elements` returns `[]` at the deadline.
- On a native app, `click`, `send_keys` and `clear` wait until the element is displayed and enabled. `submit` is not
  checked, and strict is off.
- `force: true` skips the wait before an action, for example `first_product.click(force: true)`. The element must still
  exist.
- If the element is not ready in time, the action raises `Raider::NotActionable`, which names the check that failed and how
  long it waited. On XCUITest, an element that the app reports as not displayed waits the whole `timeout` and then raises
  it. If you know the element works, use `force: true` for that call, or `Raider.actionability = false` for the run.
- `timeout`, `strict` and `actionability` are set under [Settings](#settings) and in the environment:
  `RAIDER_TIMEOUT=20`, `RAIDER_STRICT=0` and `RAIDER_ACTIONABILITY=0`. The first action in a run prints one line,
  `[raider] actionability on`; `RAIDER_QUIET=1` silences it.
- Set no implicit wait on the driver. It makes the checks that something is **not** on the screen wait too.
<%- else -%>
### Waiting and actionability

Pages load at their own speed, so the runtime waits for you. It checks every 0.1 seconds, for up to `timeout` seconds (10).

- `find_element` and `find_elements` wait for a match. `find_element` fails with a message such as
  `no such element: { id: "x" } (waited 10s)`. `find_elements` returns `[]` at the deadline.
- Before an action, the runtime waits until the element is ready:

| Action | Waits until the element is |
|--------|----------------------------|
| `click` | visible, enabled, not inert, stable, receives events |
| `send_keys` | visible, enabled, editable, not inert |
| `clear` | visible, enabled, editable, not inert |
| `submit` | visible, enabled, not inert |

- "Stable" means the element was in the same place on two checks in a row, so it is not in the middle of an animation.
  "Receives events" means nothing else covers the point where the click lands. "Inert" means the element sits inside an
  `inert` part of the page. "Editable" means the field is not read-only.
- If the element is not ready in time, the action raises `Raider::NotActionable`, which names the check that failed and how
  long it waited. If the locator matches several elements, `Raider::AmbiguousMatch` lists them.
- `force: true` skips the checks for one call: `login_button.click(force: true)`. The element must still be on the page.
- `Selenium::WebDriver::Support::Select#select_by` waits `timeout` before it raises when the option never shows up, like any
  other missing element.
- Set no implicit wait on the driver. It makes the checks that something is **not**
  on the page wait too.
- `timeout`, `strict` and `actionability` are set under [Settings](#settings) and in the environment:
  `RAIDER_TIMEOUT=20`, `RAIDER_STRICT=0` and `RAIDER_ACTIONABILITY=0`. `raider u strict off` and
  `raider u actionability off` write them to the file. `Raider.actionability = false` turns the checks off for the run. The first action in a run prints one line, `[raider] actionability on`; `RAIDER_QUIET=1` silences it.
<%- end -%>

### Assertions

The <%= mobile? ? 'screen' : 'page' %> changes after an action, so a check right after it can run too early. Raider's matchers retry until
the <%= mobile? ? 'screen' : 'page' %> matches or `timeout` runs out. Write them in a block, so the lookup runs again on every try:

```ruby
<%- if mobile? -%>
expect { pdp_page.add_to_cart_button }.to have_text('Add to Cart')
<%- else -%>
expect { account_page.welcome_message }.to have_text("Welcome back #{user['name']}")
<%- end -%>
```

A page-object method like `<%= mobile? ? 'add_to_cart_button' : 'welcome_message' %>` finds the element each time it is called. The block calls it on every try, so an element that was
redrawn is found again. `expect(<%= mobile? ? 'pdp_page.add_to_cart_button' : 'account_page.welcome_message' %>)` without a block finds it once.

The shape is the same for every matcher: `expect { <%= mobile? ? 'pdp_page.add_to_cart_button' : 'account_page.welcome_message' %> }.to have_text(…)`, with another matcher in place of `have_text`.

| Matcher | Passes when |
|---------|-------------|
| `have_text('Save')` | the text contains it (`exact: true` for all of it); a Regexp works too |
| `have_value('Ann')` | the field's `value` equals it |
| `have_attribute('type', 'text')` | the attribute equals it; with no value, the attribute is present |
| `be_visible`, `be_hidden` | the element is displayed, or is not displayed or not there |
| `be_enabled`, `be_disabled` | the element is enabled, or is not |
| `be_checked` | the checkbox or radio is selected |
| `have_count(3)` | a list has that many items: `expect { driver.find_elements(<%= mobile? ? "accessibility_id: 'row'" : "css: 'li'" %>) }.to have_count(3)` |
<%- if web? -%>
| `have_title('Done')` | the page title equals it (or matches a Regexp) |
| `have_url(/#done$/)` | the whole current URL equals it (or matches a Regexp) |
<%- end -%>
| `have_element(id: 'late-p')` | `driver` can find the element; <%= mobile? ? '`have_css` and `have_xpath` are shortcuts for web pages' : "`have_css('li')` and `have_xpath('//li')` are shortcuts" %> |

Pass `wait:` to change the time for one matcher, in seconds: `have_text('Saved', wait: 5)`. A negative check passes as soon as
the element is gone, so `expect(driver).not_to have_element(id: 'gone')` does not wait for `timeout`.

The block form works with any value matcher. `expect { value }.to eq(...)` retries until the value matches or `timeout` runs
out:

```ruby
<%- if mobile? -%>
expect { pdp_page.add_to_cart_button.text }.to eq('Add to Cart')
<%- else -%>
expect { driver.find_element(id: 'status').text }.to eq('answered by /api')
<%- end -%>
```

- The failure message is RSpec's own for the last value, with the time waited in front of it, such as `(waited 10s)`. If the last
  try raised a lookup error, such as no such element, that error is reported instead. Inside `aggregate_failures` the
  `(waited Ns)` text is left out.
- RSpec's block matchers (`raise_error`, `change`, `output`, `throw_symbol` and `yield_*`) work as in RSpec: the block runs
  once and does not retry.
- `expect(value)` without a block does not retry. The value is read once, so a plain string or number that is already
  in hand cannot change.
- A block with side effects runs once per poll, so keep side effects out of it. Click first, then check.
<%- if mobile? -%>

### Gestures

`helpers/gesture_helper.rb` holds `swipe`, `scroll` and `scroll_to`. Every page includes it. They use the platform's own
`mobile:` commands, so they work on iOS and Android, and they do not wait.

```ruby
swipe('left', element)
scroll('down')
scroll_to({ accessibility_id: 'footer' }).click
```

- `swipe(direction, element = nil)` and `scroll(direction, element = nil)` take `'up'`, `'down'`, `'left'` or `'right'`. With
  an element they act on it; without one, on the whole screen.
- `scroll_to(locator)` looks for the element without waiting, scrolls the screen and looks again, up to `max_scrolls`
  times (10), then fails. Change the direction with `direction: 'up'`. It returns the element, so `scroll_to(...).click`
  works. An element that appears late near the top can be scrolled past, so first wait for something on the screen.
- `hide_keyboard`, `back` and the other Appium commands are on `driver`, and do not wait.
<%- end -%>


### Running the tests

<%- if mobile? -%>
Start Appium and a simulator or emulator first (see "Setting up Appium" below). Then:
<%- else -%>
Install the gems once with `bundle install`. Then:
<%- end -%>

```bash
bundle exec rspec                                 # every test
bundle exec rspec spec/<%= examples? ? (mobile? ? 'pdp_page_spec.rb' : 'login_page_spec.rb') : 'checkout_page_spec.rb' %>          # one file
<%- unless mobile? -%>
HEADLESS=true bundle exec <%= test_cmd %>                   # without a browser window
<%- end -%>
```

<%- if mobile? -%>
- To run tests in parallel, each process needs its own device or simulator. This project does not set that up, so
  run the tests one at a time unless you add it.
<%- else -%>
- In parallel, with the `parallel_tests` gem in the Gemfile: `bundle exec parallel_rspec spec/`. Each process opens its own
  browser.
<%- end -%>
- `RETRY_COUNT=2 bundle exec rspec` runs each test up to 2 times, so a failing test gets one more try before it
  counts as failed.

### Running in CI

<%- if azure? && mobile? -%>
There is no Azure pipeline for mobile projects yet; see [CI](#ci).
<%- elsif azure? -%>
`azure-pipelines.yml` runs the suite headless on Azure Pipelines; see [Azure Pipelines](#azure-pipelines) to set it up.
<%- elsif mobile? -%>
`.github/workflows/appium_pipeline.yml` runs the suite on GitHub Actions on every push to `main` or `master` and on
every pull request. It downloads the test app and starts a simulator or emulator and Appium.
<%- else -%>
`.github/workflows/test_pipeline.yml` runs the suite headless on GitHub Actions on every push to `main` or `master`
and on every pull request, and uploads `raider-results/`.
<%- end -%>

Every run writes a report; see [Reporting](#reporting).
<%- if web? -%>
To log in once and reuse the session across tests, see [Reusing the login](#reusing-the-login).
<%- end -%>
<%- if mobile? -%>

### Setting up Appium

1. Install Appium and its driver: `npm install -g appium`, then
   `appium driver install <%= android? ? 'uiautomator2' : 'xcuitest' %>`<%= cross_platform? ? ' and `appium driver install uiautomator2`' : '' %>.
2. Download the example [app](https://github.com/RaiderHQ/raider_test_app) and put it in the project folder.
   `app` in `config/capabilities.yml` is its path<%= cross_platform? ? ' (`RaiderTestApp.app` for iOS, `app-release.apk` for Android)' : (ios? ? ' (`RaiderTestApp.app`)' : ' (`app-release.apk`)') %>.
3. Start a<%= android? ? 'n Android emulator' : (ios? ? 'n iOS simulator' : ' simulator or an emulator') %> that matches `deviceName` and `platformVersion`.
4. Start the Appium server with `raider u start_appium` (plain `appium`), and keep it running while the tests run.
   The tests connect to it at `http://127.0.0.1:4723`, Appium's default address.
<%- end -%>

<%- if network_helper? -%>
## Mocking network requests

Sometimes a test should not depend on a real server: you want an empty list, an error page, or no images at all.
`NetworkHelper` lets a test answer a request with your own response, or block it, before it leaves the browser.
It is available in every test and in the page objects, and it works on Chrome, Edge and Firefox. Safari raises
`NetworkHelper::Unavailable`, and so does a run with `BIDI=off` or a grid that cannot carry WebDriver BiDi.

The methods:

- `mock_request(url, ...)` answers matching requests with your own response. It takes `status:` (default 200, any
  number from 100 to 599), `json:` or `body:` (not both), `headers:`, `content_type:` and `method:` (for example
  `method: 'POST'`). It returns a mock, so you can look at it later.
- `block_request(url)` makes matching requests fail with a network error, as if the server were unreachable.
- `remove_mock(mock)` stops one mock. `clear_mocks` stops all of them.
- `mock.calls` is the list of requests the mock handled, oldest first, and `mock.called?` says if there was one. Each
  request has `method`, `url` and `headers` (header names are lowercase).

Set a mock up before the page makes the request, usually before you visit the page:

```ruby
# An empty list instead of the real users
mock_request('**/api/users', json: { users: [] })

# A server error, to see how the page shows it
mock_request('**/api/users', status: 500, json: { error: 'down' })

# No images, so the page loads faster
block_request('**/*.png')
```

To check that the page really made the request, keep the mock and look at its calls. Put the check in a block, because
the request may come a moment after the click, and the block is tried again until it passes:

```ruby
users = mock_request('**/api/users', json: { users: [] })
# ...open the page that loads the users...
expect { users.called? }.to be(true)
expect { users.calls.first&.method }.to eq('GET')
```

**Matching.** A URL is a glob that is matched against the whole URL, including the query string. `**` stands for any
characters, `*` for any characters except `/`, and everything else is literal. So `'**/api/users'` matches
`https://shop.test/api/users`, but not `https://shop.test/api/users?page=2`: write `'**/api/users?*'` for that. You can
also pass a Regexp. When several mocks match, the one you added last wins. Pass `method:` to match only one HTTP method.

**Name the host when you can.** A glob that starts with `scheme://host`, such as `'https://shop.test/api/users'`, tells
the browser which requests to pause. A Regexp, or a glob that starts with `**` or has no host, makes the browser pause
every request while the mock is registered, and the helper lets the ones that do not match go on. That works, but it
is slower, so prefer `'https://shop.test/api/users'` to `'**/api/users'` on pages that load a lot.

**CORS.** When the page and the mocked URL are on different origins, the helper answers the browser's preflight
request and adds the CORS headers for you, unless your mock sets them itself.

**Deciding per request.** Give `mock_request` a block to choose the answer for each request. The block gets the
request and returns a Hash with the same options as above (`status:`, `json:`, `body:`, `headers:`, `content_type:`),
`:fail` to make the request fail, or `:continue` to let it go on to the real server. To let it go on with a changed
header, return `{ continue: { headers: { 'x-test' => '1' } } }`.

```ruby
mock_request('https://shop.test/api/users', method: 'POST') do |request|
  request.headers['authorization'] ? { status: 201, json: { id: 1 } } : { status: 401 }
end
```

The block runs on a background thread while the browser waits, so keep it quick and do not call the driver (or any
page method) inside it.

**Cleanup and errors.** Mocks are cleared after each test, so one test never affects the next. If a block raises, or
returns something that is not a response, the request fails and the test fails with `NetworkHelper::MockError`. A bad
`status:` or a pattern that is neither a String nor a Regexp raises right where you call `mock_request`.

A mocked request shows up as "Mocked" in the [Trace replay](#trace-replay), so you can see which requests the test
answered itself.

### Removing the network helper

The network helper is ordinary code in your project, so you can take it out. To do that:

1. Delete `helpers/network_helper.rb`.
2. Remove `include NetworkHelper` from `page_objects/abstract/page.rb`, `config.include(NetworkHelper)` from `helpers/spec_helper.rb`, and the
   `require_relative` lines that load `network_helper`.
3. In the after hook (`helpers/spec_helper.rb`), remove the `NetworkHelper.finish` line and `raise mock_error`.
4. In `helpers/driver_helper.rb`, remove the `NetworkHelper` half of the `bidi` condition, so it reads
   `bidi = defined?(TraceHelper) && TraceHelper.bidi?(load_browser)`.
5. Delete any `mock_request` and `block_request` calls in your tests.

<%- end -%>
<%- if screenshot_helper? -%>
## Screenshot comparison

Use it when "does the page still look right?" is the question: a layout, a chart, a component. `match_screenshot` takes
a screenshot of the page and compares it with a picture you approved earlier (the baseline). If they differ, the test fails.

```ruby
match_screenshot('home')                                     # the viewport
match_screenshot('cart/summary', element: cart_summary)      # one element
match_screenshot('home', max_diff_pixels: 50, timeout: 5)    # allow a few pixels, wait up to 5 s
```

A name is letters, digits, `-` and `_`, with `/` for folders, up to 100 characters, and can be used once per test.

**Where baselines live.** `screenshots/baselines/<browser>/<name>.png`, for example
`screenshots/baselines/chrome/home.png`. Commit them: they are part of your tests. Each browser has its own folder.

**First run.** With no baseline, the call saves the screenshot as the baseline and passes. Look at the new file, and
commit it if it is what you want. On CI (when `CI` is set or the reporter detects a CI system) a missing baseline fails the test
and writes nothing, so a forgotten file never goes unnoticed.

**After a change you wanted.** Run `SCREENSHOTS=update bundle exec rspec`. Every comparison that differs
rewrites its baseline and passes. Review the changed PNGs in your diff before you commit them.

**When it fails.** The failure message names the baseline and says how many pixels differ. The report gets three pictures,
`comparison:<name>:expected`, `:actual` and `:diff` (differences in red), and the trace has a "match_screenshot" step that shows the actual screenshot. The
comparison retries until it matches or `timeout:` (the wait timeout by default) runs out, so a page that is still settling does not fail it.

**Tolerances.** Set them for the project in `config/config.yml` under `screenshots:`, or for one call as arguments:

- `threshold` (default 0.2): how different a pixel must be to count, from 0 (exact) to 1.
- `max_diff_pixels` (default 0): how many pixels may differ.
- `max_diff_ratio` (default none): or the share of all pixels that may differ, for example `0.001`.

Anti-aliasing is not ignored (unlike Playwright's default), so for text-heavy areas raise `threshold` or `max_diff_pixels`.

A comparison passes when the differing pixels are within `max_diff_pixels` or `max_diff_ratio`. A picture with a different
size always fails, and the message gives both sizes.

**Generate baselines where CI runs.** Fonts and rendering differ between machines, so a baseline made on your laptop may not match CI.
A Retina screen is the usual case: it makes 2x pictures, and a 1x CI machine fails with a size change that names the
device pixel ratio. Create the baselines in the same environment that checks them (run the suite once with `SCREENSHOTS=update` there and commit the files).

### Removing screenshot comparison

The helper is ordinary code in your project, so you can take it out. To do that:

1. Delete `helpers/screenshot_helper.rb` and the `screenshots/` folder.
2. Remove `include ScreenshotHelper` from `page_objects/abstract/page.rb`, `config.include(ScreenshotHelper)` from `helpers/spec_helper.rb`, and the
   `require_relative` lines that load `screenshot_helper`.
3. Remove the `ScreenshotHelper.reset` line from the before hook (`helpers/spec_helper.rb`).
4. Remove the `screenshots:` block from `config/config.yml` and `gem 'chunky_png'` from the `Gemfile`.
5. Delete any `match_screenshot` calls in your tests.

<%- end -%>
<%- if web? -%>
## Reusing the login

<%- if examples? -%>
Tag an example `:logged_in` and the suite logs in once, saves the
session, and restores it for the next tagged example or run instead of filling in the login form again. The generated
account example shows it. If the saved session no longer works, the real login runs again and the state is saved anew.
<%- else -%>
`helpers/session_state.rb` logs in once, saves the session, and restores it for the next test or run instead of
filling in the login form again. If the saved session no longer works, the real login runs again and the state is
saved anew.

This project has no login page yet. Once you have one (`raider g page login`), add a hook so that every
example tagged `:logged_in` starts logged in. In `helpers/spec_helper.rb`, require your pages next to
the other `require_relative` lines, then add the hook inside `RSpec.configure`:

```ruby
require_relative '../page_objects/pages/login'
require_relative '../page_objects/pages/dashboard'

config.before(:each, :logged_in) do
  dashboard = Dashboard.new(driver)
  SessionState.new(driver).reuse(valid: -> { dashboard.tap(&:visit).logged_in? }) do
    login = Login.new(driver)
    login.visit
    login.login(ENV.fetch('TEST_USER'), ENV.fetch('TEST_PASSWORD'))
  end
end
```

`Login` and `Dashboard` stand for your own pages: `valid:` checks that a restored session is still logged in (here a
`logged_in?` method you write on the page), and the block does the real login.
<%- end -%>

For another role, call it yourself with its own name:

```ruby
SessionState.new(driver, name: 'admin').reuse(valid: -> { dashboard.tap(&:visit).admin? }) do
  # the real login for the admin
end
```

- `login_state: max_age:` in `config/config.yml` is how many seconds a saved login stays valid (default 3600).
- `RAIDER_LOGIN_STATE=off` always does the real login and saves nothing; `RAIDER_LOGIN_STATE=refresh` ignores the saved
  file, logs in and saves again.
- The state lives in `.raider/auth/` and is git-ignored. It holds live session tokens, so it must not be committed,
  cached or uploaded as a CI artifact. Nothing in it is ever printed.

<%- end -%>
## Reporting

Every run writes machine-readable results to `raider-results/` (including `run.json`) and a browsable report,
`raider-results/index.html`. Open it in a browser after a run; `bundle exec raider-reporter report` rebuilds it.

To upload runs to Raider, set these environment variables before running the suite.
`RAIDER_TOKEN` and `RAIDER_ENDPOINT` come from your Raider Hideout server (its setup page, or `bin/rails hideout:setup` /
`hideout:token`):

```bash
export RAIDER_TOKEN=your-token
export RAIDER_ENDPOINT=https://hideout.example.com
```

Without `RAIDER_TOKEN`, nothing is uploaded and the suite output is unchanged. Failure screenshots are attached to the
report and to Raider. See the [raider_reporter README](https://github.com/RaiderHQ/raider_reporter) for details.

### Video replay

Every test is recorded, and the video is kept only for failing tests (on the last retry attempt). It is attached
to the report as `video` and saved under `raider-results/attachments/`, next to the failure screenshot.
<%- if mobile? -%>
Appium records the device screen and the video is attached as `video.mp4`.
<%- else -%>
The video is a `video.webm` built from page screenshots with ffmpeg.
<%- end -%>

<%- if single_platform? -%>
- Turn video off for one run with `VIDEO=off bundle exec rspec`, or for the project with
  `raider u video off`, which creates `config/config.yml` with a `video:` section (`raider u video on` turns it back
  on).
<%- else -%>
- Video is on by default: `video:` in `config/config.yml` (`enabled: true`, `fps: 5`, `max_width: 1280`).
- Turn it off for one run with `VIDEO=off bundle exec rspec`, or for the project with
  `raider u video off` (`raider u video on` turns it back on).
<%- end -%>
<%- unless mobile? -%>
- ffmpeg is downloaded on first use, checked against a pinned SHA-256 and cached in `~/.cache/raider`
  (`%LOCALAPPDATA%\raider` on Windows). `raider u video install` downloads it ahead of time (CI does this before the
  tests and caches the folder), and `raider u video status` prints the binary in use.
- `RAIDER_FFMPEG_PATH` uses your own ffmpeg (it needs libvpx) instead of the download, and `RAIDER_CACHE_DIR` moves
  the cache.
<%- if azure? -%>
- `azure-pipelines.yml` sets `RAIDER_CACHE_DIR` to `$(Pipeline.Workspace)/.cache/raider`, the folder its `Cache@2`
  step keeps between runs.
<%- end -%>
- The RaiderHQ `ffmpeg-7.1` release that the download comes from is not published yet. Until it is, set
  `RAIDER_FFMPEG_PATH` to get videos; without it the suite runs as usual and prints one `[video]` line.
- If ffmpeg is missing or recording fails, the suite still runs and its exit code is unchanged; you get one `[video]`
  line on stderr and no video.
- Known limits: a frame is taken after each page load, click and text change, plus one at the end of a failing test,
  so changes the page makes on its own between actions show up in the next frame. There is one recorded driver per
  process.
<%- else -%>
- Appium's screen recording needs `ffmpeg` on the Appium server host's `PATH` (this machine only when Appium runs
  here): always for iOS (XCUITest), and for Android when a recording is longer than 180 s. Without it, recording
  fails with one `[video]` line and the tests run as usual.
<%- if cross_platform? -%>
- Each test records for at most 1800 s on iOS and 180 s on Android, so Android needs no ffmpeg on the Appium host.
<%- elsif ios? -%>
- Each test records for at most 1800 s.
<%- else -%>
- Each test records for at most 180 s, so the Appium host needs no ffmpeg; longer tests keep only their first 180 s.
<%- end -%>
  Set `time_limit` (seconds) under `video:` in `config/config.yml`<%= single_platform? ? ' (create it if it is missing)' : '' %> to change it; on Android, more
  than 180 s needs ffmpeg on the Appium host.
- `raider u video install` and `raider u video status` manage the ffmpeg that web projects use, cached in
  `~/.cache/raider` (override with `RAIDER_FFMPEG_PATH` or `RAIDER_CACHE_DIR`); they do not install ffmpeg on the
  Appium host. The RaiderHQ `ffmpeg-7.1` release it comes from is not published yet.
- If recording fails, the suite still runs and its exit code is unchanged; you get one `[video]` line on stderr and
  no video.
<%- end -%>

<%- if trace_helper? -%>
### Trace replay

Every test records a trace, and the trace is kept only for failing tests (on the last retry attempt). A trace is a step-by-step
record of the test: each action and page load, with the locator, how long it waited, a screenshot and the page HTML
after it, the network requests, and the browser console. It is saved as `raider-results/attachments/<n>-trace.zip`,
next to the failure screenshot and video, and attached to the report as `trace`.

The trace records `click`, `send_keys`, `clear` and `submit` with their locators (`by_role` as
`button, name: Save`), and the time each one waited. Chained finds show the element they search in. Reads such as
`text`, `attribute` and `displayed?`, `find_element`, `hover`, `drag_and_drop` and matcher assertions are not steps.

- Keep the trace of passing tests too with `TRACE=on bundle exec rspec`. Capture nothing for one run with
  `TRACE=off bundle exec rspec`, or for the project with `raider u trace off` (`raider u trace on` turns it back on).
  `TRACE=off` turns tracing off but BiDi stays on, so [network mocking](#mocking-network-requests) still works;
  `BIDI=off` turns BiDi off, which also drops the network and console entries from a trace.
  `raider u trace on` and `raider u trace off` write `enabled:` under `trace:` in `config/config.yml`.
- Open a trace in Raider Desktop, or from the test's page on Hideout when the run is uploaded.
- Cost: a trace takes one screenshot and one page HTML for every step that changes the page (a click, typing,
  a page load), including in passing tests, whose traces are then thrown away. If that slows your suite down,
  switch tracing off as above.
- Caps, under `trace:` in `config/config.yml`: `max_steps` (200) keeps the newest steps, and `max_mb` (20) keeps the
  zip under that size. When a cap is reached, the HTML of the oldest steps is dropped first, then their
  screenshots, then the oldest network and console entries. The last 10 steps always keep theirs.
- Network requests and console messages are captured only on Chrome, Edge or Firefox. Other browsers, such as
  Safari, get a trace with steps, screenshots and HTML only. Capture is passive: the trace listens to the browser and
  never changes a request.
- The trace helper never fails a test. When it has to leave something out, such as network on a browser without
  BiDi or a screenshot it could not take, you get at most one `[trace]` notice on stderr for the run. If capturing
  breaks, you get one more `[trace]` line ending in "tracing disabled for this run", and no more traces for that run.

**Treat a trace like the video: it can contain secrets.** What is hidden: values typed into password fields
(`type=password`, or an `autocomplete` that contains "password"), and any typed value equal to a `RAIDER_SECRET_*`
environment variable. Everything else is kept: other typed values, page text, URLs with their query strings, and
console text. So a secret typed into an ordinary field, or shown on the page, ends up in the trace. Failure messages
can contain typed text too, because an assertion message is kept as it is. Request headers, cookies and request or
response bodies are never captured. Do not share or upload traces from runs that handle real credentials.

**Removing the trace helper.** The trace helper is ordinary code in your project. Delete `helpers/trace_helper.rb` and
the `require_relative` lines that load `trace_helper`: in `helpers/driver_helper.rb`. The other lines that use it
(in `helpers/spec_helper.rb` and the driver helper's listeners) only run when `TraceHelper` is defined,
so they can stay. Remove the `trace:` block from `config/config.yml` if you like.

<%- end -%>
<%- unless azure? && mobile? -%>
### Quarantine

CI runs the suite through `bundle exec raider-reporter exec -- …` with `RAIDER_QUARANTINE` set. Tests quarantined in
Raider still run and are still reported (as `quarantined`), and a build whose only failures are quarantined stays green.
Local runs keep quarantine off. To turn it off in CI, remove `RAIDER_QUARANTINE` from the pipeline.

<%- end -%>
<%- unless azure? && mobile? -%>
### Quality gate

The `<%= azure? ? 'raider_gate' : 'raider-gate' %>` CI job runs `bundle exec raider-reporter gate` after the tests and
fails the pipeline when the Raider quality gate fails. It is off until you turn it on:

- set the CI variable `RAIDER_GATE=1` (<%= azure? ? 'a pipeline variable' : 'a repository variable under Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions → Variables' %>);
- turn on rules under the project's Gate page in Raider: a minimum pass rate, no new failures against the default
  branch, no new flaky tests;
- optionally require the `raider/gate` check in branch protection.

`raider-reporter gate` exits 1 when the gate fails, naming the failed rules, and 2 when it gets no verdict (timeout,
Raider unreachable, token rejected, build not found). `RAIDER_GATE_TIMEOUT` sets how long it waits, in seconds
(default 600). The job stays off by default because a project without `RAIDER_TOKEN`, or a pull request from a fork,
would exit 2 on every run.

Parallel test jobs that upload to one build must each set `RAIDER_SHARD_TOTAL` (the number of jobs) and
`RAIDER_SHARD_INDEX` (1 to that number), so the gate waits for all of them instead of judging the first one to finish.

<%- end -%>
<%- if azure? && mobile? -%>
## CI

Azure Pipelines template for mobile projects is not available yet. Use GitHub Actions (`ci:github`) or write your own pipeline; see the raider_reporter README for the Azure setup.

<%- elsif azure? -%>
## Azure Pipelines

`azure-pipelines.yml` runs the suite on `ubuntu-latest` and, on pull requests, comments the Raider results.

- Create a pipeline from `azure-pipelines.yml`, then add `RAIDER_TOKEN` (mark it secret) and `RAIDER_ENDPOINT` as pipeline variables.
- Contribute to pull requests: grant the pipeline's build service identity (`<Project> Build Service (<Organization>)`) the "Contribute to pull requests" permission on the repository, so `raider-reporter comment` can post the comment and status.
- Azure Repos ignores `pr:` triggers. Add this pipeline as a Build validation branch policy so pull request builds (and the Raider comment) run.
- Keep the `raider_comment` job in the same stage as the `tests` job.
- Pull request comments work for Azure Repos only; a GitHub-hosted repo built by Azure Pipelines gets results uploaded but no PR comment.
- Self-hosted agent? Replace `vmImage: ubuntu-latest` with `pool: { name: Default }`.

<%- end -%>
### Sponsors

This project is tested with BrowserStack.
